---
title: Deterministic Server-Side Rendering for Typed Documents
type: paper
format: ieee
author: Jane Researcher; John Engineer
affiliation: CloudSurf Software LLC, Research Division
abstract: We present SurfDoc, a typed document format that compiles a single source to multiple academic artifacts. Our renderer produces byte-identical Typst and LaTeX output, enabling reproducible scientific publishing without external layout engines. We evaluate the approach across IEEE, ACM, and generic article formats and show that deterministic charts and diagrams embed cleanly into both PDF and HTML targets.
date: 2026-06-27
---

# Introduction

Reproducible document generation is a recurring need in scientific computing.
Prior approaches couple authoring tightly to a single backend [@smith2020].
SurfDoc decouples the source from the target, drawing on the data-science
tooling tradition surveyed by [@jones2019] and the scaling results of
[@garcia2022].

# Method

Our pipeline parses one `.surf` source into a typed block tree, then renders it
through profile-specific templates. The citation engine resolves inline cites
such as [@smith2020; @garcia2022] and emits a numbered reference list.

::figure[src=architecture.png caption="SurfDoc rendering pipeline" width=80%]
::

The throughput of the renderer across input sizes is shown below.

::chart[type=line title="Render throughput (docs/sec)"]
Size | Typst | LaTeX
S    | 1200  | 1500
M    | 980   | 1240
L    | 760   | 1010
::

# Results

A practitioner overview of transformer-based classification appears in
[@lee2021, p. 4]. Across all tested formats the output was deterministic.

